SUMMARY:Yom Kippur (Day of Atonement)
DESCRIPTION:History: Yom Kippur\, on 10 Tishrei\, is the holiest day of the
  Jewish year\, closing the Ten Days of Awe with a full day of fasting\, pr
 ayer\, and repentance.\nTraditions: A 25-hour fast from sundown\, white ga
 rments and the tallit worn through Kol Nidre and the day's services\, and 
 the final blast of the shofar at Ne'ilah as the gates close.\nFeasting: A 
 generous pre-fast meal of chicken soup and kreplach\, then a break-fast of
  bagels\, lox\, kugel\, blintzes\, and sweet tea once three stars appear.\
 n\nIcon: 📖\nCategory: religious\n\nAgenda: https://mss.castalia.institu
 te/agenda/yom-kippur-day-of-atonement/\nRecipes:\nClassic Chicken Soup for
  Breaking the Fast: https://mss.castalia.institute/recipes/chicken-soup/\n
 Kreplach for Yom Kippur: https://mss.castalia.institute/recipes/kreplach/\
 nClassic New York-Style Bagels: https://mss.castalia.institute/recipes/bag
 els/\nYom Kippur Lox and Bagel Platter: https://mss.castalia.institute/rec
 ipes/lox/\nSweet Noodle Kugel: https://mss.castalia.institute/recipes/kuge
 l/\nClassic Cheese Blintzes: https://mss.castalia.institute/recipes/blintz
 es/\nClassic Sweet Tea: https://mss.castalia.institute/recipes/sweet-tea/
